{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Moana ( ) is a 2016 American 3D computer-animated musical fantasy-adventure film produced by Walt Disney Animation Studios and released by Walt Disney Pictures.","title":"Moana (2016 film)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is the 56th Disney animated feature film.","title":"Moana (2016 film)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Directed by Ron Clements and John Musker, co-directed by Don Hall and Chris Williams, the film introduces Auli'i Cravalho as Moana and features the voices of Dwayne Johnson, Rachel House, Temuera Morrison, Jemaine Clement, Nicole Scherzinger, and Alan Tudyk.","title":"Moana (2016 film)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The film features music written by Lin-Manuel Miranda, Opetaia Foa'i, and Mark Mancina.","title":"Moana (2016 film)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pooh's Heffalump Movie is a 2005 American animated film produced by DisneyToon Studios and released by Walt Disney Pictures, featuring characters from A. A. Milne's "Winnie-the-Pooh" stories.","title":"Pooh's Heffalump Movie"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This film features songs by Carly Simon.","title":"Pooh's Heffalump Movie"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day is a 1968 animated featurette based on the third, fifth, ninth, and tenth chapters from "Winnie-the-Pooh" and the second, eighth, and ninth chapters from "The House at Pooh Corner" by A. A. Milne.","title":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The featurette was produced by Walt Disney Productions and released by Buena Vista Distribution Company on December 20, 1968 as a double feature with "The Horse in the Gray Flannel Suit".","title":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" This was the second of the studio's Winnie the Pooh shorts.","title":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was later added as a segment to the 1977 film "The Many Adventures of Winnie the Pooh".","title":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The music was written by Richard M. Sherman and Robert B. Sherman.","title":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It was notable for being the last animated short produced by Walt Disney, who died during its production.","title":"Winnie the Pooh and the Blustery Day"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Pooh's Heffalump Halloween Movie is an 2005 American animated direct-to-video film produced by DisneyToon Studios, featuring the characters from Winnie the Pooh franchise.","title":"Pooh's Heffalump Halloween Movie"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This is the first Pooh movie since the death of Paul Winchell and the last in which John Fiedler provides Piglet's voice, as Fiedler died in 2005.","title":"Pooh's Heffalump Halloween Movie"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Nikita Hopkins the voice of Roo was replaced by Jimmy Bennett.","title":"Pooh's Heffalump Halloween Movie"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It followed by a television film produced by Walt Disney Television Animation, "Pooh's Super Sleuth Christmas Movie", released on November 20, 2007, an animated feature served as the episodes of television series "My Friends Tigger & Pooh".","title":"Pooh's Heffalump Halloween Movie"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Piglet's Big Movie is a 2003 American animated musical comedy-drama adventure film produced by Walt Disney Feature Animation and animated by Walt Disney Animation (Japan).","title":"Piglet's Big Movie"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was released by Walt Disney Pictures on March 21, 2003.","title":"Piglet's Big Movie"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The film is based upon the characters in the "Winnie-the-Pooh" books written by A. A. Milne.","title":"Piglet's Big Movie"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It is the second in a recent series of theatrically released "Winnie the Pooh" films, preceded by "The Tigger Movie" (2000) and followed by "Pooh's Heffalump Movie" (2005).","title":"Piglet's Big Movie"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In the